  {"t": "Choosing gold objects for a BI team",
   "q": "The BI team needs (a) daily revenue by region, read by 50 dashboards; (b) a cleaned customer list where analysts should see the latest data with no copy; (c) a live feed of payment events; (d) a hand-maintained table of regional targets. Which object for each?",
   "steps": [
    "(a) is an aggregate read often: a materialized view. It stores the result and refreshes incrementally when the query and sources allow, which needs serverless; on classic compute every refresh recomputes fully.",
    "Schedule it in the definition (SCHEDULE EVERY 1 HOURS, or TRIGGER ON UPDATE to refresh when sources change), or refresh it from a pipeline.",
    "(b) wants no copy and always-current results: a view. It runs its query on every read, so keep it simple or point it at a materialized view.",
    "(c) is append-only and arrives continuously: a streaming table, which processes each new event once.",
    "(d) is edited by people: a regular managed table that you update with INSERT or MERGE.",
    "Grant the BI group SELECT on the gold objects plus USE CATALOG and USE SCHEMA, rather than on the silver tables underneath.",
   ],
   "answer": "(a) Materialized view on a schedule. (b) View. (c) Streaming table. (d) Managed table. Grant SELECT on these to the BI group.",
   "code": "CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW main.gold.daily_revenue\nSCHEDULE EVERY 1 HOURS\nAS SELECT order_date, region, sum(amount) AS revenue\nFROM main.silver.orders\nGROUP BY order_date, region;"},
  {"t": "Quality rules: warn, drop, fail or quarantine",
   "q": "A pipeline builds silver_payments. Rules: amount must be positive (bad rows should be removed but counted), currency must be present (keep the rows but track how many lack it), and payment_id must never be null (stop everything if it is). Analysts also want to see the removed rows.",
   "steps": [
    "Positive amount: ON VIOLATION DROP ROW. Rows are removed and the count shows in the pipeline's data quality metrics.",
    "Currency present: an expectation with no ON VIOLATION clause. That is warn, the default: rows stay and are counted.",
    "payment_id not null: ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE. The update stops and its write rolls back; no metrics are recorded because the update failed, so read the error in the event log.",
    "Dropped rows are gone from silver. To let analysts see them, add a separate quarantine table that selects the rows failing the rule. Write each table in its own flow rather than splitting inside one foreachBatch.",
    "Outside pipelines, Delta CHECK and NOT NULL constraints reject a write that breaks them.",
   ],
   "answer": "DROP ROW for amount, a plain EXPECT for currency, FAIL UPDATE for payment_id, plus a quarantine table built from the inverse of the amount rule.",
   "code": "CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE silver_payments (\n  CONSTRAINT positive_amount EXPECT (amount > 0) ON VIOLATION DROP ROW,\n  CONSTRAINT has_currency EXPECT (currency IS NOT NULL),\n  CONSTRAINT has_id EXPECT (payment_id IS NOT NULL) ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE\n) AS SELECT * FROM STREAM(bronze_payments);\n\nCREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE quarantine_payments\nAS SELECT * FROM STREAM(bronze_payments) WHERE NOT (amount > 0);"},
